[
  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186133828,
      "id": "16a9183a-68b4-5bad-b2d8-3bcf6597f263",
      "title": "Evaluating Line Integrals of a Vector-Valued Functions Over Plane Curves"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "e6d9d7c2-12ac-5ec7-8494-f31e7797261d",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Evaluate ${\\int}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\text{d}\\mathbf{r}$ given that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=(- x + 2)\\,\\mathbf{i}+(- y - 2)\\,\\mathbf{j}$ and $C$ is the path $\\mathbf{r}(t)=(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,\\mathbf{i}+(t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in[0,\\pi].$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=\\int_a^b \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot \\mathbf{r}^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\quad y=t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\\\ \\begin{aligned} &=(- x + 2)\\,\\mathbf{i}+(- y - 2)\\,\\mathbf{j} \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&\\left(- \\left(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) + 2\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i} \\\\ &{} +\\left(- \\left(t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) - 2\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&(- t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 2)\\,\\mathbf{i} \\\\ &{} +(- t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 2)\\,\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}^{\\prime}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\\mathbf{r}^{\\prime}(t) \\\\ \\begin{aligned} &=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\mathbf{j} \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,\\mathbf{i} \\\\ &{} +\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&(- t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,\\mathbf{i} \\\\ &{} +(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot \\mathbf{r}^{\\prime}(t) \\\\ \\begin{aligned} &=\\begin{aligned}&\\left({\\begin{aligned}&(- t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 2)\\,\\mathbf{i} \\\\ &{} +(- t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 2)\\,\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}}\\right) \\\\ &{} \\cdot\\left({\\begin{aligned}&(- t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,\\mathbf{i} \\\\ &{} +(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}}\\right)\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&(- t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 2) \\\\ &{} \\cdot(- t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}) \\\\ &{} +(- t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 2) \\\\ &{} \\cdot(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&\\left({\\begin{aligned}&t^{2} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}  \\\\ &{} - t \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\end{aligned}}\\right) \\\\ &{} +\\left({\\begin{aligned}&- t^{2} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - t \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}  \\\\ &{} - 2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\end{aligned}}\\right)\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&- 2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - t  \\\\ &{} - 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\\int_C \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\ \\begin{aligned} &=\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot \\mathbf{r}^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\left({\\begin{aligned}&- 2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - t  \\\\ &{} - 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\end{aligned}}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\left[{- \\frac{t^{2}}{2} - 2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}}\\right]_{0}^{\\pi} \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&(\\left(- \\frac{\\pi^{2}}{2}\\right)+(0)+(- 2 \\pi)) \\\\ &{} -((0)+(0)+(0))\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=(- 2 \\pi - \\frac{\\pi^{2}}{2})-(0) \\\\ &=- \\frac{\\pi \\left(\\pi + 4\\right)}{2}\\end{aligned}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nThe offset terms integrate by the reverse product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(- \\frac{t^{2}}{2} - 2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) \\\\ \\begin{aligned} &=\\begin{aligned}&- 2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - t  \\\\ &{} - 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\\end{gathered}\n$$",
